---
import OnThisPage from '../components/OnThisPage.astro';
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Scribble from '../components/Scribble.astro';
import theRule from '../assets/about/the-rule-excerpt.jpg';
import alisonBechdel from '../assets/about/alison-bechdel.jpg';
import { loadFilms } from '../lib/films.mjs';

const ext = { rel: 'noopener noreferrer', target: '_blank' } as const;
// The statement's numbers follow the shelf, the way the footer's do
const films = loadFilms();
const failShare = Math.round((films.filter((f) => !f.bechdel.pass).length / films.length) * 100);

// Page vs screen labels, precision on the test films (docs/page-vs-screen/METHODS.md, section 6.1)
const ACCURACY = [
	{ label: 'Heard as written', share: 95 },
	{ label: 'Cut', share: 81 },
	{ label: 'Not in the script', share: 73 },
	{ label: 'Changed', share: 68 },
];

// The page's sections, for the contents list at the top
const CONTENTS = [
	{ id: 'where-it-comes-from', label: 'Where it comes from' },
	{ id: 'what-it-cant-tell-you', label: "What it can't tell you" },
	{ id: 'how-we-check', label: 'How we check a film' },
	{ id: 'page-vs-screen', label: 'Page vs screen' },
	{ id: 'artist-statement', label: 'Artist statement' },
	{ id: 'why-we-built-it', label: 'Why we built it' },
	{ id: 'read-more', label: 'Read more' },
	{ id: 'thanks', label: 'Thanks' },
];
---

<Base
	title="About the Bechdel test"
	description="Where the Bechdel test comes from, what it can and can't tell you about a film, why Laurel and Joe Karlsson built bechdel.io, and who helped."
	nav="about"
	structuredData={{
		'@type': 'AboutPage',
		name: 'About bechdel.io',
		url: 'https://bechdel.joekarlsson.com/about/',
		author: [
			{ '@type': 'Person', name: 'Laurel Karlsson' },
			{ '@type': 'Person', name: 'Joe Karlsson' },
		],
	}}
>
	<div class="page">
		<h1 class="page-title">About the Bechdel test</h1>
		<p class="page-lead">A film passes if it has at least two women in it, who talk to each other, about something besides a man.</p>
		<OnThisPage items={CONTENTS} />

		<div class="prose">
			<h2 id="where-it-comes-from">Where it comes from</h2>
			<figure class="strip paper">
				<Picture src={theRule} alt="A panel from the comic: one woman tells another the three rules a movie has to meet before she'll see it." formats={['avif', 'webp']} width={390} height={260} />
				<figcaption>
					A panel from <cite>The Rule</cite>, <cite>Dykes to Watch Out For</cite>, 1985. © Alison Bechdel, shown here for
					commentary. <a href="https://dykestowatchoutfor.com/the-rule/" {...ext}>Read the whole strip on her site</a>.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<p>
				The test comes from Alison Bechdel's 1985 comic strip <cite>Dykes to Watch Out For</cite>, in the strip called
				<cite>The Rule</cite>. One character explains that she only watches movies that meet three requirements. Bechdel credits
				the idea to her friend Liz Wallace, who was inspired by Virginia Woolf's essay <cite>A Room of One's Own</cite> (1929).
			</p>
			<figure class="portrait">
				<Picture src={alisonBechdel} alt="Alison Bechdel smiling, at the Boston Book Festival" widths={[300, 600]} sizes="(max-width: 600px) 60vw, 300px" formats={['avif', 'webp']} loading="lazy" />
				<figcaption>
					Alison Bechdel at the Boston Book Festival. Photo by
					<a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/chasblackman/6247651679/" {...ext}>Chase Elliott Clark</a>,
					<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" {...ext}>CC BY 4.0</a>, cropped.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<p>
				What began as a joke in an independent comic became one of the best-known measures of how films represent women. It took
				off in the 2010s, when Swedish cinemas began using it to rate films and the press started tracking how Hollywood did. By
				showing how few films have even one conversation between women about something other than a man, it became a tool for
				feminist film criticism.
			</p>

			<h2 id="what-it-cant-tell-you">What it can't tell you</h2>
			<p>
				The test is simple on purpose, and that's its power. It also means it can't capture everything about how a film treats
				women.
			</p>
			<h3>What it measures</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>Whether women have speaking roles</li>
				<li>Whether women talk to each other</li>
				<li>Whether those conversations go beyond men</li>
			</ul>
			<h3>What it doesn't</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>How well the women are written, or how they grow</li>
				<li>Whether they have agency</li>
				<li>Race, class, sexuality and disability</li>
				<li>What the conversation means to the film</li>
				<li>Who made the film: director, writer, cinematographer</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A film can pass while leaning on harmful stereotypes, and a film can fail while giving us a powerful woman.
				<cite>Gravity</cite> (2013) fails because it has only one woman, yet she carries the whole film. Some films pass on one
				throwaway line between background characters.
			</p>
			<p>
				That's why each film page goes past pass or fail: who gets the lines, who's behind the camera, and how old the stars
				are. bechdeltest.com readers give a second opinion. Use the test to start a conversation, not to end one.
			</p>

			<h2 id="how-we-check">How we check a film</h2>
			<p>
				A parser reads the film's script scene by scene. It counts the women and men, finds the scenes where two women speak to
				each other, and checks whether those scenes name a man. That gives each film its Bechdel score, from 0 to 3. It also counts
				who speaks, how much, and when, which feeds the rest of each film page.
			</p>
			<p>
				Scripts are formatted in many ways, so some counts are off. When we can't tell who a line belongs to, or a script didn't
				parse, the film's page says so.
			</p>

			<figure class="how" aria-labelledby="how-caption">
				<figcaption id="how-caption" class="sr-only">How bechdel.io checks a film, step by step</figcaption>
				<ol class="steps">
					<li class="step paper">
						<span class="step-n">1</span>
						<h3>The script</h3>
						<p>We start from the film's shooting script, from the Internet Movie Script Database.</p>
					</li>
					<li class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"><Scribble kind="arrow" class="down" /></li>
					<li class="step paper">
						<span class="step-n">2</span>
						<h3>Scenes</h3>
						<p>Every scene heading, like <span class="slug">INT. THE BAR - DAY</span>, starts a new scene.</p>
					</li>
					<li class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"><Scribble kind="arrow" class="down" /></li>
					<li class="step paper">
						<span class="step-n">3</span>
						<h3>Who's talking</h3>
						<p>Each line goes to the character who says it. The cast list tells us which characters are women.</p>
					</li>
					<li class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"><Scribble kind="arrow" class="down" /></li>
					<li class="step paper rules-step">
						<span class="step-n">4</span>
						<h3>The three rules, in order</h3>
						<ol class="rule-chain">
							<li>Two women?</li>
							<li>Talking to each other in a scene?</li>
							<li>With no man named?</li>
						</ol>
						<p>Each rule met adds a point. A film that fails one rule isn't checked against the next.</p>
						<p class="result"><span class="stamp-small">Bechdel score, 0 to 3</span></p>
					</li>
				</ol>
				<div class="alongside">
					<div class="side">
						<h3>A second opinion</h3>
						<p>bechdeltest.com readers rate the film too, so you can see where they agree with us.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="side">
						<h3>The counts</h3>
						<p>Who gets the lines and the words, how often two women or two men talk, and who worked behind the camera.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="side">
						<h3>The film</h3>
						<p>Posters, cast, plot, ratings and awards come from TMDB and OMDb.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</figure>

			<h2 id="page-vs-screen">Page vs screen</h2>
			<p>
				A script is a plan. Lines get cut, rewritten on set or made up on the spot. We line each script up with the film's
				English subtitles, word by word, and run the Bechdel test again on what's said.
			</p>
			<figure class="said">
				<figcaption class="note">One line from <cite>Pulp Fiction</cite></figcaption>
				<p><span class="said-label">Script</span> MIA: I don't know what <del>that</del> <del>was.</del> <del>Music</del> <del>and</del> drinks!</p>
				<p><span class="said-label">Film</span> I don't know what <ins>you</ins> <ins>call</ins> <ins>that.</ins> Drinks!</p>
				<p class="note"><del>Struck</del> words are cut. <ins>Underlined</ins> words aren't in the script.</p>
			</figure>
			<p>
				Most films have several sets of subtitles. We use up to nine, and a word counts as heard when most of them have it.
			</p>
			<figure class="accuracy">
				<figcaption class="note">How often each label is right, checked against 800 labelled lines from 20 films</figcaption>
				<ul class="hbars wide-labels">
					{ACCURACY.map((r) => (
						<li>
							<div class="hb-row" role="img" aria-label={`${r.label}: right ${r.share}% of the time`}>
								<span class="hb-label">{r.label}</span>
								<span class="hb-track"><span class="hb-bar neutral" style={`width: ${r.share}%`} /></span>
								<span class="hb-value">{r.share}%</span>
							</div>
						</li>
					))}
				</ul>
				<p class="note">
					<a href="https://github.com/JoeKarlsson/bechdel-test/blob/main/docs/page-vs-screen/METHODS.md" {...ext}>The full method and its accuracy</a>
				</p>
			</figure>
			<h3>What it can't tell you</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>
					"Not in the script" means not in the words of this draft. An ad-lib, a later rewrite and a reshoot look the same,
					and so does a line said in new words, which is about a third of them.
				</li>
				<li>Subtitlers shorten long lines, which can look like a cut. We haven't measured how often.</li>
				<li>Some scripts online were typed up from the finished film. They match it by design, so we leave them out.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="artist-statement">Artist statement</h2>
			<p>
				Bechdel.io checks movie scripts against the Bechdel Test. The test comes from a 1985 Alison Bechdel comic strip, and it asks three things: are there at least two women, do they talk to each other, and do they talk about something other than a man.
			</p>
			<p>
				It sorts a wall of movies most people have already seen into pass and fail. {failShare}% of the {films.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} films on the shelf fail, and a lot of them are favorites.
			</p>
			<p>
				We released it as open source because we saw it as feminist activism.
			</p>
			<p class="credit">
				Joe Karlsson, with Laurel Karlsson. The statement is also on
				<a href="https://www.joekarlsson.com/work/bechdel-io/" {...ext}>bechdel.io’s page on joekarlsson.com</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="why-we-built-it">Why we built it</h2>
			<p>
				This digital humanities project, by Joe and me, comes from a shared love of film, feminism and what technology can do. It
				brings together my work as an English scholar and Joe's as a software engineer.
			</p>
			<p>
				It started when I asked Joe for advice on a few ideas for a digital humanities project. I wanted to do something with
				feminist film analysis, but my lack of coding experience held me back, and I couldn't find a data mining tool that did what
				I needed. Joe offered to build one from scratch. After brainstorming several ideas, we settled on this one.
			</p>
			<p>
				We also chose it because so few digital humanities projects look at film. Data mining tools are often used on literature
				and other texts, but rarely on film. Anyone can sit through a film with a notebook and pencil to see if it passes the
				Bechdel test, but it's slow. Automating it means we can look at a director's whole filmography, an actress's body of work,
				or every film from one year.
			</p>
			<p>
				It was an experiment in collaboration, too. We built it without ever sharing a room: I was in Boulder, Colorado, and Joe
				was in Honolulu, Hawaii. We relied on FaceTime, iMessage and Google Docs, and it worked.
			</p>
			<p>
				We see this tool as a form of feminist activism, so it's open source and free for anyone to use.
				<a href="https://github.com/JoeKarlsson/bechdel-test" {...ext}>The code is on GitHub</a>.
			</p>
			<p>Laurel and Joe</p>
			<p>
				P.S. If you have ideas for where this should go next,
				<a href="https://github.com/JoeKarlsson/bechdel-test/issues" {...ext}>we'd love to hear them</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="read-more">Read more</h2>
			<ul class="links">
				<li><a href="https://dykestowatchoutfor.com/the-rule/" {...ext}><cite>The Rule</cite></a>, the 1985 strip where the test began, on Alison Bechdel's site</li>
				<li><a href="https://alisonbechdel.com/" {...ext}>Alison Bechdel</a>, her books and comics</li>
				<li><a href="https://bechdeltest.com/" {...ext}>Bechdel Test Movie List</a>, readers rating thousands of films against the test</li>
				<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bechdel_test" {...ext}>The Bechdel test on Wikipedia</a>, its history, variants and critics</li>
				<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Room_of_One%27s_Own" {...ext}><cite>A Room of One's Own</cite></a>, Virginia Woolf's 1929 essay behind the idea</li>
				<li><a href="https://feministfrequency.com/video/the-bechdel-test-for-women-in-movies/" {...ext}><cite>The Bechdel Test for Women in Movies</cite></a>, a short video from Feminist Frequency</li>
				<li><a href="https://dhq.digitalhumanities.org/vol/8/4/000190/000190.html" {...ext}><cite>Visualizing and Analyzing the Hollywood Screenplay with ScripThreads</cite></a>, Digital Humanities Quarterly</li>
				<li><a href="https://aclanthology.org/L16-1147/" {...ext}><cite>OpenSubtitles2016: Extracting Large Parallel Corpora from Movie and TV Subtitles</cite></a>, the paper behind the subtitle collection we compare scripts with</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="thanks">Thanks</h2>
			<p>Thank you to:</p>
			<ul class="links">
				<li>
					<a href="https://bechdeltest.com/" {...ext}>The Bechdel Test Movie List</a> and its readers, whose ratings are the
					second opinion on every film page
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://imsdb.com/" {...ext}>The Internet Movie Script Database</a>, where the scripts come from, gathered with
					<a href="https://github.com/JoeKarlsson/movie-script-scraper" {...ext}>movie-script-scraper</a>
				</li>
				<li><a href="https://www.themoviedb.org/" {...ext}>The Movie Database (TMDB)</a>, for posters, backdrops and cast lists</li>
				<li><a href="https://www.omdbapi.com/" {...ext}>The Open Movie Database (OMDb)</a>, for plots, ratings and awards</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://opus.nlpl.eu/" {...ext}>OPUS</a> and the subtitlers of opensubtitles.org, for the subtitles we compare
					scripts with
				</li>
				<li>Chase Elliott Clark, for the photo of Alison Bechdel</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="tmdb">
				<img src="/tmdb-logo.svg" alt="The Movie Database (TMDB)" width="155" height="20" loading="lazy" />
				<span>This product uses the TMDB API but is not endorsed or certified by TMDB.</span>
			</p>
			<p><a href="/">Browse the films</a></p>
		</div>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.said p {
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
		font-size: var(--text-lead);
	}
	.said del {
		color: var(--muted);
		text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
	}
	.said ins,
	.said .note ins {
		text-decoration: underline 3px var(--tangerine);
		text-underline-offset: 3px;
	}
	.said .note {
		margin-top: var(--space-3);
	}
	.said-label {
		display: inline-block;
		min-width: 4rem;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.accuracy {
		margin: var(--space-5) 0 var(--space-6);
	}
	figure {
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
	}
	figure img {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
	}
	figcaption {
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
		font-size: var(--text-small);
	}
	.strip {
		max-width: 430px;
		padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
		transform: rotate(-1.5deg);
	}
	.portrait {
		max-width: 300px;
	}
	.portrait figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	@media (min-width: 1180px) {
		.portrait {
			float: right;
			width: 300px;
			margin: 0 calc(-1 * (300px + var(--space-6))) var(--space-4) 0;
		}
	}
	/* How it works: four steps down the left, what runs alongside on the right */
	.how {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: var(--space-5);
		margin: var(--space-6) 0;
	}
	@media (min-width: 900px) {
		.how {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 2fr);
			width: min(1100px, calc(100vw - 2 * var(--gutter)));
		}
	}
	.steps {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
	}
	.step {
		position: relative;
		padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-4) var(--space-4) 4.5rem;
	}
	.step:nth-child(4n + 1) {
		transform: rotate(-0.8deg);
	}
	.step:nth-child(4n + 3) {
		transform: rotate(0.8deg);
	}
	.step h3 {
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
	}
	.step p {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.step-n {
		position: absolute;
		left: var(--space-3);
		top: var(--space-3);
		font-family: var(--poster);
		font-size: 2.75rem;
		line-height: 1;
		color: var(--pink-on-chalk);
	}
	.slug {
		font-family: var(--poster);
		letter-spacing: normal;
	}
	.arrow {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		height: 3rem;
		align-items: center;
	}
	/* The crayon arrow is drawn heading down and to the left; turn it to point straight down */
	.arrow :global(.down) {
		transform: rotate(-37deg) scale(1.3);
	}
	.rule-chain {
		margin: var(--space-2) 0 var(--space-3);
		padding-left: var(--space-4);
		font-family: var(--poster);
		font-size: 1.25rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
	}
	.rule-chain li + li {
		margin-top: var(--space-1);
	}
	.result {
		margin-top: var(--space-3) !important;
	}
	.stamp-small {
		display: inline-block;
		background: var(--pink);
		color: var(--ink);
		font-family: var(--poster);
		font-size: 1.375rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
		transform: rotate(-2deg);
	}
	.alongside {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-4);
		align-self: center;
	}
	.side {
		background: var(--panel);
		padding: var(--space-4);
	}
	.side h3 {
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
		font-size: 1.375rem;
		color: var(--pink);
	}
	.side p {
		margin: 0;
	}
	code {
		font-size: 0.9em;
	}
	.links li + li {
		margin-top: var(--space-3);
	}
	.credit {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.tmdb {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		color: var(--muted);
	}
</style>
